  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1226 PM MDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nMostly dry conditions are expected for the rest of the afternoon. \nTemperatures will be warmer than yesterday, but will remain below \nnormal. There is a low chance for a few showers and thunderstorms to \ndevelop to the southeast of our forecast area tonight as weak \ndisturbance moves through aloft. An isolated shower or two could \nmake there way far enough northwest to move into Lincoln County, \nbut impacts to our forecast area are not expected.\n\nTemperatures will continue to warm tomorrow into the weekend. The \nupper level high will build north and east over the four corners \nregion, sitting over the area through Sunday. As a result, \ntemperatures will go back above normal, with highs reaching into \nthe mid 90s to around 103 across the plains for the weekend. DENs\nrecord high on Saturday will be out of reach, with a current \nrecord of 105 set in 1878, but we could go for the record high on\nSunday. The current forecast has a high of 99 on Sunday and the \ncurrent record for August 9th is 98 degrees set in 2021. Along\nwith heat, the upper level ridge will bring northwest mid and \nupper level winds, which will continuously bring smoke from fires\nin the Pacific NW into the area aloft through the end of the \nweek.\n\nThe upper level high does start to shift a bit more to the west \nSunday into early next week. Models are hinting at a return of \nmoisture sometime Sunday into the middle of next week. As the high\nshifts and weakens slightly, moisture could flow from the Pacific\ninto the forecast area aloft, bringing PWs back to near or above \nnormal by early next week. With the weakening of the ridge, a few\nupper level disturbances will have the potential to move across \nthe area starting on Sunday. The weak lift associated with the \ndisturbances could be enough to trigger a few showers and storms \nin the higher terrain on Sunday, with rain chances moving east \ninto the plains sometime late Monday into the middle of next week.\nWe'll also see a gradual cooling trend next week as moisture \nincreases, sky cover increases, and the ridge weakens.",
